WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
Application-layer pressure is difficult because the requests may resemble normal user

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.460
activity.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:07.840
The problem is not always raw volume.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.900
It may be that certain routes are expensive, certain queries miss cache,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.320
certain login or search flows trigger heavy downstream work,

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.620
or certain API patterns create queues that consume worker capacity.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.880
From the user's perspective the service is slow or unavailable.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.780
From the defender's perspective,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
the response needs application context as much as security context.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:34.580
The first defensive step is to identify expensive endpoints and critical paths before an

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.680
incident.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.740
Which pages must remain available?

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.360
Which APIs support core business functions?

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.180
Which routes are safe to cache?

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.160
Which operations should have quotas?

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.700
Which features can degrade temporarily?

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.760
Which requests touch databases, search clusters, identity providers, payment systems,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.580
or third-party services?

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.520
The answers help the team prioritize protection rather than treating every request path as

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:00.540
equal.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.600
Useful application-layer controls include caching, request limits, API quotas, queueing,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:12.100
bot controls, WAF rules, authentication hardening, circuit breakers, feature flags,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.920
and degraded modes.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.540
A cache can reduce repeated origin work.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.160
A quota can protect shared resources.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.100
A circuit breaker can stop one failing dependency from consuming the whole application.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.880
A degraded mode can preserve the most important user journey while less critical features

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.220
are paused.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.320
These are resilience patterns, not magic switches.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.060
Coordination is essential.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.680
Security may see suspicious request patterns.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.780
SRE may see saturation and error budgets.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.960
Application engineers may know which path is expensive and which configuration can safely

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.060
change.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.960
Product or business owners may need to approve a degraded mode.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.300
During an event, the strongest control may be code, configuration, edge policy,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.640
cache strategy, or a temporary business decision.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.740
Afterward,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.280
expensive paths should be reviewed and redesigned where possible so the next event is

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.940
easier to absorb.
